# SCOPIO backend -- which physical instrument is which.
#
#   cp .env.example .env        # .env is gitignored: it describes THIS rig
#   docker compose up -d        # compose reads .env automatically
#
# This is the one place the Pi's hardware wiring is written down. Nothing here
# is a secret (API keys live in secrets/api_keys.json); it is per-machine, which
# is why it is not in git.
#
# Change a value -> `docker compose up -d` again (recreates the containers).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---- Instrument addresses (VISA resource strings) -------------------------
# Leave a line commented out and the node auto-discovers by USB vendor id --
# the galvo node only ever opens a Rigol, the temperature node only ever opens a
# Wavelength, so they cannot take each other's instrument.
#
# Set one to pin it explicitly, or when the instrument is on Ethernet (pyvisa-py
# cannot scan the LAN, so those must always be named). Find addresses with
# `docker compose down && python3 scripts/list_instruments.py`.
# Do not quote the value; no trailing comment on the same line.

# Rigol DG1022Z arbitrary-waveform generator -- drives the galvo mirrors.
#GALVO_RESOURCE=USB0::0x1AB1::0x0642::DG1ZA000000000::INSTR

# Wavelength Electronics TC10 LAB -- sample temperature controller.
# Three forms, in order of what usually works on this Pi:
#   /dev/usbtmc*                    the kernel usbtmc char device. USE THIS when
#                                   the kernel driver has claimed the box, which
#                                   is what makes a plain VISA scan hang. A GLOB
#                                   is preferred over /dev/usbtmc0: the Rigol is
#                                   USB-TMC too and the numbering follows
#                                   enumeration order, so the node probes every
#                                   match and keeps the one whose *IDN? is a
#                                   Wavelength. Needs the udev rule (see udev/).
#   TCPIP::192.168.1.50::INSTR      Ethernet. Must be named -- pyvisa-py cannot
#                                   scan a LAN. Most robust if the unit is wired.
#   USB0::0x1A45::0x3101::...       plain VISA over libusb; only works if the
#                                   kernel usbtmc driver is NOT holding it.
#TCLAB_RESOURCE=/dev/usbtmc*

# Sangaboard XYZ stage (OpenFlexure) -- a serial device, not VISA.
# Auto-detection only finds boards on USB. A v0.5 HAT on the 40-pin header is a
# UART device with no USB identity, so it MUST be named:
#     SANGABOARD_PORT=/dev/serial0
# and on the Pi: enable_uart=1 in /boot/firmware/config.txt, serial LOGIN
# console OFF (raspi-config > Interface Options > Serial Port), and on a Pi 4
# dtoverlay=disable-bt so pins 8/10 get the reliable PL011 UART.
# On USB, only needed behind an unrecognised bridge: SANGABOARD_PORT=/dev/ttyACM0
#SANGABOARD_PORT=/dev/serial0

# ---- Camera frame size (optional) -----------------------------------------
# Passed to the camera service; defaults 640x480.
#CAM_W=640
#CAM_H=480
